# Hooks → app

```
claude | codex ─(hook stdin JSON)→ warroom-hook ─(Unix socket, 1 line)→ ingress.rs → IncomingSignal
   → WarRoomService::ingest → AgentPorts{provider}.translate → hooks::translate(dialect) → SessionEvent
```

Cursor and Antigravity have their own events and fields: each has its own `translate`
(`cursor.mdc`, `antigravity.mdc`) and they reuse the bridge, socket, installer and service.
Claude Code and Codex speak **the same hook protocol**: same event names, same payload fields
(`session_id`, `cwd`, `transcript_path`, `tool_name`, `tool_input`, `agent_id`…), same
`PermissionRequest` reply. So there is one translation (`hooks.rs`), one bridge, one installer
(`hook_installer.rs`); each agent only brings a `Dialect` (skill prefix, question tools, skill tool)
and a `HookSpec` (events + timeouts). Codex specifics → `codex.mdc`.

## Bridge invariants (crates/hook-bridge/src/main.rs) — never break these

- **Exit 0 always, in milliseconds when the app is down** (connect fails → exit). A slow or failing
  hook degrades every agent session on the machine.
- **stdout only carries an explicit app decision** (PermissionRequest reply). Anything else printed
  is interpreted by the agent.
- It walks `/proc` from its parent upward: hooks may run through a shell, so `getppid()` is not the
  agent. The chain gives the agent PID (liveness) and the terminal/IDE (focus).
- **Which agent**: `WARROOM_PROVIDER`, else the payload (`cursor_version` → cursor, `conversationId` →
  antigravity), else the nearest agent process (`claude`, `codex`, `cursor-agent`, `language_server`),
  else claude. Payload first: Cursor also runs Claude's hooks, from wherever it runs.
- An argument is the event name for agents whose payload lacks it (Antigravity); then the bridge
  always prints JSON (`{}`), even with the app closed.
- It only waits for a decision on `PermissionRequest` for agents that show their own dialog meanwhile
  (`DIALOG_WHILE_WAITING`: Claude). Codex runs the hook *before* its dialog: holding it would freeze
  the terminal, so for Codex the war room reports the request but can't approve it.
- Env hints captured at hook time: `TMUX`, `TMUX_PANE`, `TERM_PROGRAM`, `WARP_FOCUS_URL`, `AWR_PTY_ID`
  (set by our own PTYs). Plus `/proc/<agent>/cmdline` for the launch command.

## Socket protocol (crates/wire)

One connection per hook, one JSON line (`HookEnvelope`, `v: 1`, with `provider`). If `expects_reply`,
the bridge keeps the connection open and waits for a `HookReply` line; the app answering is how
approvals work. Old bridges sent no newline — ingress reads to newline **or** EOF. Socket:
`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/agent-war-room/ingress.sock` (dir 0700; paths must stay < 108 bytes).

## Mapping (hooks.rs)

Each hook → one `SessionEventKind` (+ `extra`: a `/skill` or `$skill` prompt is also a
`SkillInvoked`; so is the dialect's skill tool). Question tools are `AwaitingYou`. Tools with
`agent_id` belong to a subagent. `Interrupt` (Codex) → `Interrupted` (your turn, no notice).
`Notification` exists only in Claude. Unknown hooks → `Ok(None)` (ignored, not an error).

## HookFacts

A hook may bring facts besides (or instead of) a state change: model, one response's tokens, context
(`Translated.kind: None` + `facts`). The service accumulates them per session and fills what the
transcript did not say (Cursor's transcripts have no model or tokens). Derived, not persisted.

## Trigger table

| If you… | Read |
|---|---|
| touch approvals (PermissionRequest, reply, EOF detection) | `hooks-approvals.md` |
| rely on a Claude hook field, a notification type or add a hook | `hooks-claude-reference.md` |
| touch anything Codex-specific (trust prompt, rollouts, CODEX_HOME) | `codex.mdc` |
| touch Cursor (its events, the Claude hooks it also runs) | `cursor.mdc` |
| touch Antigravity (named hooks.json, events without names) | `antigravity.mdc` |
| add another agent | skill `add-provider` |

## Installer (hook_installer.rs)

Non-destructive merge into the agent's hook file (`~/.claude/settings.json`, `$CODEX_HOME/hooks.json`,
`~/.cursor/hooks.json`, `~/.gemini/config/hooks.json`) in its `HookLayout` (Grouped, Flat, Named): our entries are recognised by the `warroom-hook` marker in `command`; foreign
hooks are untouched; a `.warroom-bak` copy is written before each change; invalid JSON is refused; the
bridge is copied (tmp + rename, to avoid ETXTBSY) to `~/.local/share/agent-war-room/bin/` only if it
is a real ELF. `PermissionRequest` gets 600 s (the bridge gives up at 590 s), others 5 s (Codex caps
`SessionEnd`/`Interrupt` at 3 s and warns otherwise). `IntegrationStatus` says which agent and whether
its config folder exists (`agent_found`).
